Z||000000||Xtreme Fiero's||11-07-2000||08:25 PM||||<BR>if any one could give me an aprox HP & TQ rating and maybe a 1/4 time it would be helpful. Here is a compleete list of mods to the car as best I know.<P>1985 Fiero GT<BR>2.8L (.30 over)<BR>5-speed Isizu (SP)<BR>Fiero Shop clutch<BR>Fiero Shop Light weight Flywheel<BR>Ported Intake & heads<BR>Crane cam (mild/ streetable) not sure the grind #<BR>Cat back exhaust<BR>High Flow cat<BR>Ported manifolds<P>Poly Bushing all around (dog bone, Tranny, Cradle & A-arms)<BR>KYB shocks<BR>Adj. Camber plates<BR>Eibach (SP) sport springs<BR>225/50/15 tires<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||11-07-2000||10:52 PM||||I need more info about the cam to be more accurate, but on my version of Desktop Dyno it's showing about 180-185 hp @ 6,000 rpm and about the same tq @ 4,000. My 1/4 mile calculator puts you in the mid to upper 14's.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Xtreme Fiero's||11-08-2000||12:21 AM||||awsome thanks. Sory about the cam but I don't know the exact #'s al I know is that the original owner autocrossed tha car ALOT an rarely ever drove it on the street, and that the gut I bought it from drove it daily. so the cam can't be to aggressive. but thanks for the info just wondering, but what cam did you plug in to get thoes #'s?||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||11-08-2000||08:58 PM||||I used the higher lift Crane CompuCam specs of the 2 different Crane CompuCams grinds. The higher lift one is only recommended for manual shift cars. Since yours is a 5 speed and was being autocrossed, it's likely to have the bigger cam. This and the GM hipo cam are about the biggest cam you can use with computer controlled 2.8 engines.<P>From knowledge of others with similar mods that have been dynoed and the fact that Desktop Dyno is often a little overly generous on power readings, my guess is that you have the potential to be making at least 180 hp, assuming the head porting was done properly. Not bad at all for a little V6, especially the 180 ft/lbs of torque at 4,000 rpm. There is where your power curve is. Make sure your shifts drop you back down into your power curve each time. You'll feel that torque setting you back into the seat if you do and the other guy will see taillights.||||reg||1
